WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.988
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:18.426 --> 00:00:19.570
BRODY: I'm Alan Brody.

00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:23.020
I'm the associate provost
for the arts at MIT.

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:27.880
And I want to welcome the
MIT community and everyone

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:33.820
else as well to this
2004 Abramowitz Lecture

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:34.810
with Margaret Atwood.

00:00:34.810 --> 00:00:37.340
We're just thrilled
to have you here.

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:41.530
And we're certainly thrilled
to have Margaret here as well.

00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:46.090
Before I begin, let me
just say, unless you

00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:48.610
are a drug dealer
or an obstetrician,

00:00:48.610 --> 00:00:53.005
please turn off your
cell phones, pagers.

00:00:53.005 --> 00:00:54.202
[LAUGHTER]

00:00:54.202 --> 00:00:56.410
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:00:56.410 --> 00:00:59.145
BRODY: Thank you.

00:00:59.145 --> 00:01:03.160
The Abramowitz Lecture
Series was established at MIT

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:05.230
through the generosity
and imagination

00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:09.550
of William L.
Abramowitz, class of '35,

00:01:09.550 --> 00:01:11.980
as a memorial to his father.

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:14.200
It's been sustained
since his death

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:17.810
by the devoted interest
of his wife and children.

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:20.170
And we lost Lee,
his wife, this year.

00:01:20.170 --> 00:01:25.690
She was an extraordinary woman,
dedicated to this lecture

00:01:25.690 --> 00:01:29.680
series, dedicated to MIT,
to the memory of William,

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:33.550
and was consistently,
right up until the end,

00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:37.600
interested in this
series, and sensitive,

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:40.700
and excited about the kinds
of things we've been doing.

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:44.230
And I personally am going
to miss her a great deal.

00:01:44.230 --> 00:01:47.470
Since 1961, the
Abramowitz Series

00:01:47.470 --> 00:01:50.950
has brought renowned performing
artists and writers to MIT

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:53.380
to perform, present
public lectures,

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:57.580
and always to collaborate
with students and faculty

00:01:57.580 --> 00:01:59.810
in free programs.

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:02.890
Is Dan Epstein here?

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:06.490
There is-- I believe there's a
member of the Abramowitz family

00:02:06.490 --> 00:02:08.710
that we'd like to thank.

00:02:08.710 --> 00:02:10.060
But he didn't make it.

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:11.140
So much for commitment.

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:12.598
[LAUGHTER]

00:02:14.056 --> 00:02:15.715
AUDIENCE: Oh, my god.

00:02:15.715 --> 00:02:18.690
AUDIENCE: You might
need an overflow room.

00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:21.673
BRODY: If you're in the
overflow room, thanks so much.

00:02:21.673 --> 00:02:24.088
[LAUGHTER]

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:31.950
I want to introduce
Candis Callison, who

00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:34.930
was on the Abramowitz
Selection Committee.

00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:39.030
She's a PhD
candidate now in STS.

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:41.400
And she is personally
responsible for bringing

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:45.810
Margaret here by delivering
a hand-delivered note to her

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:48.240
at the Giller Awards.

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:51.189
So, Candice, you'll
speak about Margaret.

00:02:51.189 --> 00:02:53.634
[APPLAUSE]

00:02:57.546 --> 00:02:59.695
CALLISON: Yeah, they say
Canada is a small town,

00:02:59.695 --> 00:03:01.320
but I was really
surprised there's only

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:03.450
two degrees of
separation between myself

00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:06.180
and our esteemed guest today.

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:08.070
It's my great
privilege to introduce

00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:09.360
Margaret Atwood to you.

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:12.120
As many of you well know,
she is the prolific author

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:15.420
of 16 works of fiction, five
collections of nonfiction,

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:18.180
15 editions of poetry,
and four children's books.

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:21.810
And those are just the
major publishers' titles.

00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:23.680
Her work has won a
long list of honors,

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:25.830
including the Booker Prize
and the Giller Prize,

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:28.260
Canada's top literary award.

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:29.280
I grew up in Canada.

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:32.370
And I have to say that the
name Margaret Atwood is and was

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:36.120
synonymous with everything that
is great about Canadian fiction

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:39.790
and, indeed, what puts Canada on
the map in the literary world.

00:03:39.790 --> 00:03:42.990
But during the mid-'90s, I began
to understand her as a writer

00:03:42.990 --> 00:03:45.900
who was at work both in
the so-called real world

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:48.300
and in the fictive
worlds she was creating.

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:51.970
At that time, as a member and
past president of PEN Canada,

00:03:51.970 --> 00:03:55.530
she spoke out on behalf of Ken
Saro-Wiwa, a Nigerian writer

00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:58.500
who faced execution for his
writing and political action

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:00.130
in his own country.

00:04:00.130 --> 00:04:02.220
I remember hearing on
the radio that even

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:05.670
Margaret Atwood had spoken out
in support of this writer, who

00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:07.590
was previously an
obscure figure to me

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:09.690
and much of the Canadian public.

00:04:09.690 --> 00:04:11.790
It was a cue that we had
better sit up and take

00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:15.490
notice of what was happening
on the other side of the world.

00:04:15.490 --> 00:04:17.220
Similarly, as the
past couple of years

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:20.579
have seen America go to war
in Iraq and Afghanistan,

00:04:20.579 --> 00:04:23.040
her voice could be found in
newspapers and publications

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:25.560
like The Nation, calling
readers to think and act

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:27.570
about the events
taking place here

00:04:27.570 --> 00:04:29.760
and on the other
side of the globe.

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:32.640
Perhaps this is why nestled
in amongst her list of prizes

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:34.830
for literature and
writing are awards

00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:37.740
that speak on both her
affect on and involvement

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:41.280
with the nonfictive world,
awards like Ms. Magazine's

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:43.380
Woman of the Year,
Humanist of the Year,

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:45.960
and the Order of Canada,
Canada's top honor to those

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:49.550
who have immeasurably
contributed to our society.

00:04:49.550 --> 00:04:51.300
As I was preparing to
introduce her today,

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:53.880
I went online and
read the many rave

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:55.380
reviews for Oryx and Crake.

00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:57.450
And in amongst them
on a message board,

00:04:57.450 --> 00:04:59.790
I found a posting from an
individual with the summary

00:04:59.790 --> 00:05:02.400
line "shockingly relevant."

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:04.510
This perhaps sums up
her work in general

00:05:04.510 --> 00:05:07.260
and specifically in
regards to Oryx and Crake.

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:09.150
The posting went on
to praise this novel

00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:12.150
for the creation of an
all-too-recognizable world

00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:15.960
in which characters grapple with
moral and scientific dilemmas.

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:19.110
Again, it seems that it is a
cue to sit up and start thinking

00:05:19.110 --> 00:05:21.180
with and through
science and technology

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:24.150
about the world we are
collectively creating.

00:05:24.150 --> 00:05:27.390
And perhaps there is no
better crowd than ours at MIT

00:05:27.390 --> 00:05:30.150
that understands the relevancy
of thinking about society

00:05:30.150 --> 00:05:32.170
through this sort of lens.

00:05:32.170 --> 00:05:34.020
So without further
ado, here to talk more

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:37.216
about her work and her latest
novel, Margaret Atwood.

00:05:37.216 --> 00:05:40.192
[APPLAUSE]

00:05:56.560 --> 00:05:57.890
ATWOOD: Thank you very much.

00:05:57.890 --> 00:06:00.440
It's a great pleasure
to be here today.

00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:03.040
I have been at MIT before.

00:06:03.040 --> 00:06:07.870
As I recall, that event
took place in a corridor,

00:06:07.870 --> 00:06:11.700
and I'm glad we all
have chairs today.

00:06:14.230 --> 00:06:20.230
I'm here to talk about my
novel called Oryx and Crake.

00:06:20.230 --> 00:06:24.460
But before I do that, I'll give
you a little bit of background

00:06:24.460 --> 00:06:27.730
because people are asking
me things like, how did you

00:06:27.730 --> 00:06:28.990
do the research?

00:06:28.990 --> 00:06:33.640
And why were you interested
in this subject, and so forth.

00:06:33.640 --> 00:06:35.800
It is a book about that
wonderful place called

00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:39.070
the future in which we can
invent because none of us

00:06:39.070 --> 00:06:42.010
have ever been there
and come back yet.

00:06:46.100 --> 00:06:48.040
And I think the
future was what we

00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:52.210
took to when we no longer sent
fictional characters to places

00:06:52.210 --> 00:06:55.060
like Hell.

00:06:55.060 --> 00:06:56.350
Now we've got the future.

00:06:56.350 --> 00:06:59.290
For a while, we sent them
to uninhabited islands,

00:06:59.290 --> 00:07:01.390
but we ran out of those.

00:07:01.390 --> 00:07:05.860
And sometimes we send
them to other universes,

00:07:05.860 --> 00:07:09.850
but I can't do other universes.

00:07:09.850 --> 00:07:13.070
So it's this universe,
a little bit further on.

00:07:13.070 --> 00:07:15.340
And as for The
Handmaid's Tale, I

00:07:15.340 --> 00:07:20.570
put nothing in it that does not
have its corresponding clipping

00:07:20.570 --> 00:07:25.360
in the ominous brown
research box in the cellar.

00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:28.240
That is, nothing is
absolutely pure invention.

00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:33.000
Although, I have to admit, I
cranked a few things up a bit.

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:37.640
However, the spider
goat is with us today.

00:07:37.640 --> 00:07:40.060
And so is the
luminous green rabbit.

00:07:40.060 --> 00:07:43.030
I will not hear gasps
of shock from this crowd

00:07:43.030 --> 00:07:44.420
because you already know that.

00:07:44.420 --> 00:07:46.190
But some people go, oh, no.

00:07:46.190 --> 00:07:51.910
Anyway, I, in fact, grew
up amongst the scientists.

00:07:51.910 --> 00:07:54.520
My father was a biologist.

00:07:54.520 --> 00:07:57.370
He was an entomologist.

00:07:57.370 --> 00:08:00.940
These are noteworthy for
producing weird, writer

00:08:00.940 --> 00:08:01.750
offspring.

00:08:04.540 --> 00:08:07.850
And indeed, a lot of
biologists write themselves,

00:08:07.850 --> 00:08:10.180
and a lot of them actually read.

00:08:10.180 --> 00:08:15.060
And my dad was a great reader.

00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:16.030
He loved history.

00:08:16.030 --> 00:08:17.020
He loved fiction.

00:08:17.020 --> 00:08:22.420
And he was known to burst into
long snatches of memorized Sir

00:08:22.420 --> 00:08:26.770
Walter Scott poems
and also to give us

00:08:26.770 --> 00:08:31.660
quizzes on Sherlock Holmes,
as in what Sherlock Holmes

00:08:31.660 --> 00:08:36.094
story contains the bell
pull and the snake?

00:08:36.094 --> 00:08:37.350
AUDIENCE: The Speckled Band.

00:08:37.350 --> 00:08:37.933
ATWOOD: Right.

00:08:40.559 --> 00:08:42.720
So that's how I grew up.

00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:45.600
And I thought I was
going to be a biologist.

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:48.060
I was headed in that direction.

00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:50.250
My brother actually became one.

00:08:50.250 --> 00:08:52.140
He became a marine biologist.

00:08:52.140 --> 00:08:54.840
And from there, he
went into neuroscience,

00:08:54.840 --> 00:08:57.910
and now he's pretty
much with the synapse.

00:08:57.910 --> 00:09:01.530
And when I published my
first book of poetry,

00:09:01.530 --> 00:09:05.170
he wrote me a congratulatory
letter that said,

00:09:05.170 --> 00:09:09.420
"Congratulations on publishing
your first book of poetry.

00:09:09.420 --> 00:09:13.740
I used to do that kind of thing
myself when I was younger."

00:09:13.740 --> 00:09:16.115
[LAUGHTER]

00:09:20.400 --> 00:09:25.320
So I've always kept up with
the pop-science reading.

00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:27.060
And by pop-science
reading, I mean

00:09:27.060 --> 00:09:30.950
the kind where you don't
have to do the math yourself.

00:09:30.950 --> 00:09:32.730
I like other people
to do the math

00:09:32.730 --> 00:09:35.410
and then tell me what
they've found out.

00:09:35.410 --> 00:09:38.250
But I think that growing
up with a scientist

00:09:38.250 --> 00:09:39.610
gives you a couple of things.

00:09:39.610 --> 00:09:41.730
Number one, you're
very observant.

00:09:41.730 --> 00:09:43.620
You observe details.

00:09:43.620 --> 00:09:46.210
It's not just a tree.

00:09:46.210 --> 00:09:48.180
It's a certain kind of tree.

00:09:48.180 --> 00:09:52.060
And it also makes
you quite skeptical.

00:09:52.060 --> 00:09:54.610
I don't mean that you're
a cynic or a pessimist.

00:09:54.610 --> 00:09:57.030
I mean that you question
things because, as you know,

00:09:57.030 --> 00:10:01.890
in science, an experiment
has to be repeatable and get

00:10:01.890 --> 00:10:03.760
the same results.

00:10:03.760 --> 00:10:06.270
So you are always asking
things like, well,

00:10:06.270 --> 00:10:08.010
how did they come
to that conclusion?

00:10:08.010 --> 00:10:09.810
How did they do the experiment?

00:10:09.810 --> 00:10:13.080
And since I spent some time
in market research as well,

00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:16.560
I have the same
skepticism about polls.

00:10:16.560 --> 00:10:19.410
I want to know what the
question was and also

00:10:19.410 --> 00:10:24.060
what the question was that was
asked just before that question

00:10:24.060 --> 00:10:28.390
because that can skew
the results as well.

00:10:28.390 --> 00:10:34.860
Oryx and Crake, its structure is
like that of the Iliad, a book

00:10:34.860 --> 00:10:36.680
it resembles in no other way.

00:10:36.680 --> 00:10:44.250
But that is, it begins
in the middle of events,

00:10:44.250 --> 00:10:46.410
and then we go back
in time to find out

00:10:46.410 --> 00:10:48.630
how we got to that
midpoint, and then

00:10:48.630 --> 00:10:53.280
we go forward in time to find
out what the protagonist will

00:10:53.280 --> 00:10:54.600
do next.

00:10:54.600 --> 00:10:56.430
And at the midpoint
of the story,

00:10:56.430 --> 00:10:59.010
the protagonist, who
began life as a boy

00:10:59.010 --> 00:11:03.480
called Jimmy has changed
his name to Snowman.

00:11:03.480 --> 00:11:08.310
That is not as in
"Frosty the" but

00:11:08.310 --> 00:11:13.080
as in the Abominable Snowman,
a creature that may or may not

00:11:13.080 --> 00:11:17.940
exist and may or may
not be semi-human,

00:11:17.940 --> 00:11:21.480
which is kind of the way
he's feeling about himself

00:11:21.480 --> 00:11:23.520
when the story opens.

00:11:23.520 --> 00:11:27.300
He is living in a tree
where he sleeps at night.

00:11:27.300 --> 00:11:31.590
This is because of the
new and unusual life forms

00:11:31.590 --> 00:11:35.100
that are running around
on the ground at night.

00:11:35.100 --> 00:11:38.130
And you'll be happy
to know that I've

00:11:38.130 --> 00:11:40.320
provided him with duct tape.

00:11:40.320 --> 00:11:42.652
[LAUGHTER]

00:11:43.152 --> 00:11:45.990
[APPLAUSE]

00:11:45.990 --> 00:11:50.730
There will be duct
tape in the future.

00:11:50.730 --> 00:11:53.070
It is with the aid
of this duct tape

00:11:53.070 --> 00:11:56.340
that he has constructed
the platform on which he

00:11:56.340 --> 00:11:59.190
spends his nights.

00:11:59.190 --> 00:12:02.190
He kind of wishes he might be
able to have a fire up there,

00:12:02.190 --> 00:12:03.600
but he can't.

00:12:03.600 --> 00:12:05.820
And apart from the
duct tape, he's

00:12:05.820 --> 00:12:09.040
not really a handy kind of guy.

00:12:09.040 --> 00:12:12.390
He's not a kind of Boy
Scout sort of person.

00:12:12.390 --> 00:12:15.570
He is, therefore, unable to
dig the hole in the ground

00:12:15.570 --> 00:12:19.500
with the pointed stake on
which to impale small game.

00:12:19.500 --> 00:12:20.700
He can't do that.

00:12:20.700 --> 00:12:24.210
But he is able to
wield a can opener.

00:12:24.210 --> 00:12:30.210
This is a skill I would
advise everyone to acquire.

00:12:32.720 --> 00:12:34.800
And with the aid
of this can opener,

00:12:34.800 --> 00:12:38.460
he has been opening cans that
he has found left around,

00:12:38.460 --> 00:12:40.890
and he has been
eating the contents

00:12:40.890 --> 00:12:43.950
because there are
no other people, as

00:12:43.950 --> 00:12:46.470
such, in his vicinity.

00:12:46.470 --> 00:12:49.000
Something has happened to them.

00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:51.540
We find out in the
course of the plot what,

00:12:51.540 --> 00:12:55.200
but I would never think
of blowing it for you.

00:12:55.200 --> 00:12:58.740
Because, shocking as it
may seem to me, some of you

00:12:58.740 --> 00:13:00.530
may not yet have read this book.

00:13:03.960 --> 00:13:07.230
There are, however, some
people-like creatures

00:13:07.230 --> 00:13:12.150
living nearby along with
the luminous rabbits.

00:13:12.150 --> 00:13:14.400
We've got the luminous
rabbits already,

00:13:14.400 --> 00:13:19.680
by the way, and some
other animals that

00:13:19.680 --> 00:13:21.540
have been invented.

00:13:21.540 --> 00:13:25.860
But these new people
have been improved.

00:13:25.860 --> 00:13:27.810
And I'll tell you some
of the improvements.

00:13:27.810 --> 00:13:31.200
I think some of them would be
quite good, thus disproving

00:13:31.200 --> 00:13:33.790
the question, surely,
you're against science.

00:13:33.790 --> 00:13:34.290
No.

00:13:34.290 --> 00:13:36.520
I think it's great
so long as it is

00:13:36.520 --> 00:13:39.460
for improving types of things.

00:13:39.460 --> 00:13:42.955
For instance, these people
have built-in sunblock.

00:13:42.955 --> 00:13:44.080
I think that would be good.

00:13:47.490 --> 00:13:50.640
That means they don't
need the textile industry,

00:13:50.640 --> 00:13:55.080
or Vogue magazine, or shopping.

00:13:55.080 --> 00:13:56.550
They don't need
any of those things

00:13:56.550 --> 00:13:59.040
because they don't
do any clothes.

00:13:59.040 --> 00:14:02.560
They have an extra layer of skin
on the bottoms of their feet,

00:14:02.560 --> 00:14:05.080
giving them a kind of
Birkenstock effect.

00:14:07.750 --> 00:14:10.460
And they have built-in
mosquito repellent,

00:14:10.460 --> 00:14:14.860
which I think would
also be a major plus.

00:14:14.860 --> 00:14:17.950
They do smell like
walking citrus fruits,

00:14:17.950 --> 00:14:21.940
but that wouldn't be too bad.

00:14:21.940 --> 00:14:26.050
In addition to that,
they're not only vegetarian.

00:14:26.050 --> 00:14:28.540
Better, they will
never need agriculture

00:14:28.540 --> 00:14:33.340
because they can eat
leaves, unlike you and me.

00:14:33.340 --> 00:14:35.000
We can't digest them.

00:14:35.000 --> 00:14:38.480
But these people can--
and grass as well.

00:14:38.480 --> 00:14:40.540
This means that their
digestive systems would

00:14:40.540 --> 00:14:42.740
have to have been modified.

00:14:42.740 --> 00:14:45.430
Now, there are two
possible models.

00:14:45.430 --> 00:14:48.160
One would have been
the cow, but that

00:14:48.160 --> 00:14:52.300
would have had a less
aesthetically pleasing effect,

00:14:52.300 --> 00:14:55.570
and these people are
very good looking.

00:14:55.570 --> 00:14:57.430
So instead, they've
been modified

00:14:57.430 --> 00:15:01.780
in the direction of the rabbit.

00:15:01.780 --> 00:15:04.460
If you saw the, I
think a few months ago,

00:15:04.460 --> 00:15:08.200
National Geographic
with the new, big wheel

00:15:08.200 --> 00:15:10.510
of life about who's
related to whom,

00:15:10.510 --> 00:15:12.430
you will have noted
that we are more

00:15:12.430 --> 00:15:16.700
closely related to the rabbit
than previously thought.

00:15:16.700 --> 00:15:18.800
So maybe it wouldn't
be too hard.

00:15:18.800 --> 00:15:21.760
Now, there are some side effects
to having the digestive system

00:15:21.760 --> 00:15:22.420
of a rabbit.

00:15:22.420 --> 00:15:24.820
And you will come across
those in the book.

00:15:24.820 --> 00:15:28.720
But it's a small price to pay.

00:15:32.590 --> 00:15:34.720
The other modification
they have which

00:15:34.720 --> 00:15:37.675
I think would be very useful
is that they can purr.

00:15:41.758 --> 00:15:44.005
The purring is not
to make them cuter.

00:15:47.680 --> 00:15:49.810
There is, in fact,
grounds to believe--

00:15:49.810 --> 00:15:51.920
I did say I cranked
some things up a bit.

00:15:51.920 --> 00:15:56.770
But there's grounds to believe
that the purring of the cat

00:15:56.770 --> 00:15:58.000
has a couple of functions.

00:15:58.000 --> 00:16:00.010
We know that cats purr
when they're happy,

00:16:00.010 --> 00:16:04.480
but we also know that they
purr when they're in pain.

00:16:04.480 --> 00:16:07.330
And if you have
ever had a cat, you

00:16:07.330 --> 00:16:10.390
will know that if you
have been injured or ill,

00:16:10.390 --> 00:16:13.060
your cat is very likely
to get up on top of you

00:16:13.060 --> 00:16:16.780
and purr over your
injured part or, indeed,

00:16:16.780 --> 00:16:20.670
over your whole self if you've
got something like the flu.

00:16:20.670 --> 00:16:22.900
And you will also know
that if a person walks

00:16:22.900 --> 00:16:25.840
into your house who
hates and fears cats,

00:16:25.840 --> 00:16:30.100
your cat will go
immediately to that person.

00:16:30.100 --> 00:16:34.180
And so, possibly, the purring
is the self-healing aid,

00:16:34.180 --> 00:16:37.180
and your cat is going over to
that person out of a desire

00:16:37.180 --> 00:16:40.780
to be helpful and kind--

00:16:40.780 --> 00:16:44.290
the only altruistic thing a
cat has ever been known to do.

00:16:47.770 --> 00:16:51.130
So the people in this book
have got purring because they

00:16:51.130 --> 00:16:54.920
don't Band-Aids or any other--

00:16:54.920 --> 00:16:58.090
they don't have hospitals,
but they do have purring.

00:16:58.090 --> 00:17:01.310
So they can purr to self-heal.

00:17:01.310 --> 00:17:03.910
But the very, very, very best
thing of all that they have--

00:17:03.910 --> 00:17:07.300
and I think this would
be such an improvement--

00:17:07.300 --> 00:17:12.250
instead of being intermittently
monogamous the way we are,

00:17:12.250 --> 00:17:17.450
they are, instead, seasonal
like most other animals.

00:17:17.450 --> 00:17:21.130
So they are either in season, in
which case they're interested,

00:17:21.130 --> 00:17:24.849
or they're not in season,
in which case they're not.

00:17:24.849 --> 00:17:28.540
And to make things
even more clear,

00:17:28.540 --> 00:17:33.820
they, like many another
animal, are color-coded so

00:17:33.820 --> 00:17:38.920
that, when they're in season,
parts of them turn blue.

00:17:38.920 --> 00:17:40.630
Think how useful that would be.

00:17:43.330 --> 00:17:47.680
No more, no means
no-- no means yes.

00:17:47.680 --> 00:17:49.990
No more, I'm washing my hair.

00:17:49.990 --> 00:17:55.780
And I'll also be washing
my hair next Friday.

00:17:55.780 --> 00:17:57.910
No more unrequited love.

00:17:57.910 --> 00:18:01.630
No more pain and
anguish over this issue.

00:18:01.630 --> 00:18:06.040
You're either in season
or you're not in season.

00:18:06.040 --> 00:18:08.420
And no more, by the
way, pair-bonding.

00:18:11.860 --> 00:18:12.700
So there they are.

00:18:12.700 --> 00:18:16.990
They will never write Othello.

00:18:16.990 --> 00:18:19.660
In fact, they will
never write anything

00:18:19.660 --> 00:18:22.180
because they can't write.

00:18:22.180 --> 00:18:26.740
These people live at some
distance from our protagonist.

00:18:26.740 --> 00:18:27.580
Why is that?

00:18:27.580 --> 00:18:31.780
First of all, to them, he looks
pretty strange and monstrous.

00:18:31.780 --> 00:18:35.410
But also, for him,
they're pretty boring.

00:18:35.410 --> 00:18:38.530
So he actually doesn't want to
spend too much time with them

00:18:38.530 --> 00:18:41.920
because they don't
understand him at all,

00:18:41.920 --> 00:18:48.920
and they don't share many of
the same items of interest.

00:18:48.920 --> 00:18:52.900
This is his predicament at
the beginning of the book.

00:18:52.900 --> 00:18:56.490
The two characters
on the front, Oryx,

00:18:56.490 --> 00:18:59.920
who is a female person, and
Crake, who is a male person,

00:18:59.920 --> 00:19:02.650
are dead by the time
the book begins.

00:19:02.650 --> 00:19:04.400
I think this is a
great advantage.

00:19:07.300 --> 00:19:09.340
What it means is
that we see them

00:19:09.340 --> 00:19:13.900
only through Jimmy Snowman
and his memories of them.

00:19:13.900 --> 00:19:15.460
They happen to have
been the two most

00:19:15.460 --> 00:19:18.890
important people in his life.

00:19:18.890 --> 00:19:22.750
Oryx was the person he
loved and idealized--

00:19:22.750 --> 00:19:24.280
he's quite a romantic--

00:19:24.280 --> 00:19:28.750
ever since he first saw her
on an internet porn site.

00:19:28.750 --> 00:19:32.080
And Crake is his best friend.

00:19:32.080 --> 00:19:35.230
With him, he went
to high school.

00:19:35.230 --> 00:19:37.780
Now, at the end of high
school, their paths

00:19:37.780 --> 00:19:42.790
diverged for a while because
Jimmy was not a numbers person.

00:19:42.790 --> 00:19:44.710
Jimmy was a words person.

00:19:44.710 --> 00:19:47.680
Crake, on the other
hand, was good at both.

00:19:47.680 --> 00:19:52.810
So Crake got to go off to the
very well-funded Watson Crick

00:19:52.810 --> 00:19:57.070
Institute, and Jimmy was
stuck with the crumbling,

00:19:57.070 --> 00:20:05.710
falling-apart Martha Graham
Academy, the graduates of which

00:20:05.710 --> 00:20:11.200
are all going to end up in
advertising in the future.

00:20:11.200 --> 00:20:14.230
Because in the future, the
arts have somewhat diminished.

00:20:14.230 --> 00:20:16.600
There is, for instance,
no longer quite the film

00:20:16.600 --> 00:20:18.730
industry there is
now because people

00:20:18.730 --> 00:20:21.640
can make their own
films digitally

00:20:21.640 --> 00:20:24.550
on their very own computers.

00:20:24.550 --> 00:20:29.290
And Jimmy himself has made
quite an effective naked Pride

00:20:29.290 --> 00:20:30.713
and Prejudice.

00:20:35.550 --> 00:20:40.250
So to sum it up, it's a
joke-filmed, fun-packed,

00:20:40.250 --> 00:20:41.900
rollicking adventure--

00:20:41.900 --> 00:20:44.400
[LAUGHTER]

00:20:46.900 --> 00:20:52.580
adventure story about the
downfall of the human race.

00:20:52.580 --> 00:20:57.050
Not everybody can get those two
ideas together in their heads.

00:20:57.050 --> 00:21:02.260
But I think it's a cheering sort
of book, much in the same way

00:21:02.260 --> 00:21:05.650
that a Christmas Carol is
cheering because Scrooge gets

00:21:05.650 --> 00:21:07.900
to wake up at the end,
and he gets to say,

00:21:07.900 --> 00:21:09.700
it was all a horrible dream.

00:21:09.700 --> 00:21:13.725
I'm not really dead, and
I've got a second chance.

00:21:13.725 --> 00:21:15.850
And you can wake up at the
end of the book and say,

00:21:15.850 --> 00:21:17.080
it's only a book.

00:21:17.080 --> 00:21:18.010
We're on the way.

00:21:18.010 --> 00:21:18.550
True.

00:21:18.550 --> 00:21:23.020
But we do still have time,
and we've got a second chance.

00:21:23.020 --> 00:21:25.120
And the other
reason it's cheering

00:21:25.120 --> 00:21:28.780
is that, however awful
things may be in your life,

00:21:28.780 --> 00:21:30.340
they're much worse in the book.

00:21:34.480 --> 00:21:36.550
I always preferred
that kind of book.

00:21:36.550 --> 00:21:38.890
I don't like books at all
in which everybody's happy

00:21:38.890 --> 00:21:40.380
all the time.

00:21:40.380 --> 00:21:44.500
They depress me no end.

00:21:44.500 --> 00:21:49.840
This is the only book that
has ever received a fan

00:21:49.840 --> 00:21:53.890
letter from Kermit the Frog.

00:21:53.890 --> 00:21:55.390
Kermit sent the fan letter.

00:21:55.390 --> 00:21:57.040
It's got his picture on it.

00:21:57.040 --> 00:22:01.420
And he also sent a poem
right on the fan letter.

00:22:01.420 --> 00:22:07.060
And the poem says, "Oryx
and Crake, Oryx and Crake,

00:22:07.060 --> 00:22:13.720
a frog by a lake
reading Oryx and Crake."

00:22:13.720 --> 00:22:15.100
Now, I do not lie.

00:22:15.100 --> 00:22:16.075
This is true.

00:22:16.075 --> 00:22:18.850
And I'll tell you why
I got this fan letter.

00:22:18.850 --> 00:22:22.760
The person who edits The
Sunday Times-- sorry, yes--

00:22:22.760 --> 00:22:26.050
The Sunday Times literary
section in London, England,

00:22:26.050 --> 00:22:28.705
is a person called Erica Wagner.

00:22:28.705 --> 00:22:31.750
And Erica Wagner
grew up in New York.

00:22:31.750 --> 00:22:34.630
And her first job
at the age of eight

00:22:34.630 --> 00:22:37.720
was forging the signature
of Kermit the Frog

00:22:37.720 --> 00:22:41.560
on all of Kermit's
answers to fan letters

00:22:41.560 --> 00:22:45.160
because her parents' job was
answering all of the Muppet fan

00:22:45.160 --> 00:22:46.120
mail.

00:22:46.120 --> 00:22:47.900
This is a true story.

00:22:47.900 --> 00:22:49.120
So I think we've got--

00:22:49.120 --> 00:22:51.045
I think we put that fan
letter on our website.

00:22:53.630 --> 00:22:56.060
I'm going to read you a
tiny bit out of the book.

00:22:56.060 --> 00:22:59.080
It's not the bit I
usually choose to read.

00:22:59.080 --> 00:23:01.450
But for you, I'll read it.

00:23:01.450 --> 00:23:06.910
And it might help
explain why Kermit was so

00:23:06.910 --> 00:23:08.840
taken with this book.

00:23:12.190 --> 00:23:17.100
It's a conversation between
Crake as a teenager and Jimmy

00:23:17.100 --> 00:23:22.710
as, now, I guess, they're
both in their early 20s,

00:23:22.710 --> 00:23:25.410
and they've got together
although they're

00:23:25.410 --> 00:23:29.310
at different colleges.

00:23:29.310 --> 00:23:33.270
"'How much misery,' Crake
said, one lunchtime,

00:23:33.270 --> 00:23:36.540
'how much needless despair
has been caused by a series

00:23:36.540 --> 00:23:42.120
of biological mismatches, a
misalignment of the hormones

00:23:42.120 --> 00:23:45.930
and pheromones, resulting in
the fact that the one you love

00:23:45.930 --> 00:23:50.820
so passionately won't
or can't love you?

00:23:50.820 --> 00:23:53.670
As a species, we're
pathetic in that way,

00:23:53.670 --> 00:23:55.780
imperfectly monogamous.

00:23:55.780 --> 00:23:59.790
If we could only pair-bond
for life like gibbons or else

00:23:59.790 --> 00:24:03.090
opt for a total
guilt-free promiscuity,

00:24:03.090 --> 00:24:05.970
there would be no
more sexual torment.

00:24:05.970 --> 00:24:08.880
Better plan-- make
it cyclical and also

00:24:08.880 --> 00:24:11.820
inevitable as in
the other mammals.

00:24:11.820 --> 00:24:15.870
You'd never want someone
you couldn't have.'

00:24:15.870 --> 00:24:18.750
'True enough.' Jimmy
replied, or Jim,

00:24:18.750 --> 00:24:21.620
as he was now insisting
without results.

00:24:21.620 --> 00:24:24.240
Everyone still called him Jimmy.

00:24:24.240 --> 00:24:27.000
'But think what
we'd be giving up.'

00:24:27.000 --> 00:24:30.020
'Such as?' 'Courtship behavior.

00:24:30.020 --> 00:24:33.930
In your plan, we'd just be
a bunch of hormone robots.'

00:24:33.930 --> 00:24:37.500
Jimmy thought he should put
things in Crake's terms,

00:24:37.500 --> 00:24:41.130
which was why he said
'courtship behavior.'

00:24:41.130 --> 00:24:45.270
What he meant was the challenge,
the excitement, the chase.

00:24:45.270 --> 00:24:47.550
'There would be no free choice.'

00:24:47.550 --> 00:24:50.733
'There is courtship behavior
in my plan,' said Crake,

00:24:50.733 --> 00:24:52.275
'except that it
would always succeed.

00:24:55.300 --> 00:25:00.360
And we're hormone robots
anyway, only we're faulty ones.'

00:25:00.360 --> 00:25:04.110
'Well, what about art?' said
Jimmy, a little desperately.

00:25:04.110 --> 00:25:08.090
He was, after all, a student
at the Martha Graham Academy,

00:25:08.090 --> 00:25:14.490
so he felt some need to defend
the art and creativity turf.

00:25:14.490 --> 00:25:18.720
'What about it?' said Crake,
smiling his calm smile.

00:25:18.720 --> 00:25:21.150
'All that mismatching
you talk about,

00:25:21.150 --> 00:25:25.260
it's been an inspiration,
or that's what they say.

00:25:25.260 --> 00:25:26.610
Think of all the poetry.

00:25:26.610 --> 00:25:27.540
Think Petrarch.

00:25:27.540 --> 00:25:29.100
Think John Donne.

00:25:29.100 --> 00:25:30.570
Think The Vita Nuova.

00:25:30.570 --> 00:25:31.800
Think--'

00:25:31.800 --> 00:25:34.890
'Art,' said Crake, 'I guess
they still do a lot of jabbering

00:25:34.890 --> 00:25:37.720
about that over where you are.

00:25:37.720 --> 00:25:39.260
What is it Byron said?

00:25:39.260 --> 00:25:43.020
Who'd write if they
could do otherwise?

00:25:43.020 --> 00:25:45.420
Something like that.

00:25:45.420 --> 00:25:46.980
'That's what I
mean,' said Jimmy.

00:25:46.980 --> 00:25:49.620
He was alarmed by the
reference to Byron.

00:25:49.620 --> 00:25:53.190
What right had Crake to poach
on his own shoddy, threadbare

00:25:53.190 --> 00:25:55.140
territory?

00:25:55.140 --> 00:25:59.550
Crake should stick to science
and leave poor Byron to Jimmy.

00:25:59.550 --> 00:26:04.420
'What do you mean?' said Crake,
as if coaching a stutterer.

00:26:04.420 --> 00:26:06.570
'I mean, when you can't
get the otherwise,

00:26:06.570 --> 00:26:09.390
then wouldn't you
rather be fucking?'

00:26:09.390 --> 00:26:11.020
said Crake.

00:26:11.020 --> 00:26:13.710
He wasn't including
himself in this question.

00:26:13.710 --> 00:26:17.550
His tone was one of detached
but not very strong interest,

00:26:17.550 --> 00:26:19.320
as if he were
conducting a survey

00:26:19.320 --> 00:26:23.320
of people's less-attractive
personal habits,

00:26:23.320 --> 00:26:26.010
such as nose-picking.

00:26:26.010 --> 00:26:27.930
Jimmy found that
his face got redder

00:26:27.930 --> 00:26:32.220
and his voice got squeakier the
more outrageous Crake became.

00:26:32.220 --> 00:26:33.900
He hated that.

00:26:33.900 --> 00:26:37.020
'When any civilization is
dust and ashes,' he said,

00:26:37.020 --> 00:26:38.940
'art is all that's left over--

00:26:38.940 --> 00:26:42.420
images, words, music,
imaginative structures.

00:26:42.420 --> 00:26:45.750
Meaning, human meaning,
that is, is defined by them.

00:26:45.750 --> 00:26:48.230
You have to admit that.'

00:26:48.230 --> 00:26:51.720
'That's not quite all that's
left over,' said Crake.

00:26:51.720 --> 00:26:54.420
'The archaeologists
are just as interested

00:26:54.420 --> 00:26:57.720
in gnawed bones, and old
bricks, and ossified shit

00:26:57.720 --> 00:27:01.170
these days, sometimes
more interested.

00:27:01.170 --> 00:27:05.960
They think human meaning is
defined by those things too.'

00:27:05.960 --> 00:27:09.180
Jimmy would like to have said,
'Why are you always putting me

00:27:09.180 --> 00:27:09.960
down?'

00:27:09.960 --> 00:27:12.360
But he was afraid of
the possible answers

00:27:12.360 --> 00:27:15.790
because it's so easy
being one of them.

00:27:15.790 --> 00:27:18.570
So instead, he said, 'What
have you got against it?'

00:27:18.570 --> 00:27:20.640
"Against what?' 'Art.'

00:27:20.640 --> 00:27:23.070
'Nothing,' said Crake, lazily.

00:27:23.070 --> 00:27:25.560
'People can amuse themselves
any way they like.

00:27:25.560 --> 00:27:28.440
If they want to play with
themselves in public,

00:27:28.440 --> 00:27:31.320
whack off over doodling,
scribbling, and fiddling,

00:27:31.320 --> 00:27:32.730
that's fine with me.

00:27:32.730 --> 00:27:36.740
Anyway, it serves a
biological purpose.'

00:27:36.740 --> 00:27:40.840
'Such as?' Jimmy knew that
everything depended on keeping

00:27:40.840 --> 00:27:41.860
his cool.

00:27:41.860 --> 00:27:45.400
These arguments had to be
played through like a game.

00:27:45.400 --> 00:27:49.480
If he lost his
temper, Crake won.

00:27:49.480 --> 00:27:52.420
'The male frog in mating
season,' said Crake,

00:27:52.420 --> 00:27:54.820
'makes as much noise as it can.

00:27:54.820 --> 00:27:57.610
The females are attracted
to the male frog

00:27:57.610 --> 00:28:00.310
with the biggest,
deepest voice because it

00:28:00.310 --> 00:28:05.020
suggests a more powerful
frog, one with superior genes.

00:28:05.020 --> 00:28:08.080
Small male frogs,
it's been documented,

00:28:08.080 --> 00:28:11.170
discover that if they position
themselves in empty drain

00:28:11.170 --> 00:28:17.160
pipes, the pipe acts
as a voice amplifier,

00:28:17.160 --> 00:28:21.310
and the small frog appears
much larger than it really is.'

00:28:21.310 --> 00:28:32.130
'So?' 'So that's what art is
for the artist,' said Craig,

00:28:32.130 --> 00:28:35.580
'an empty drain
pipe, an amplifier,

00:28:35.580 --> 00:28:38.010
a stab at getting laid.'

00:28:38.010 --> 00:28:41.460
'Your analogy falls down when it
comes to female artists,' said

00:28:41.460 --> 00:28:42.180
Jimmy.

00:28:42.180 --> 00:28:44.010
'They're not in it to get laid.

00:28:44.010 --> 00:28:46.170
They'd gain no
biological advantage

00:28:46.170 --> 00:28:49.140
from amplifying themselves
since potential mates would

00:28:49.140 --> 00:28:51.270
be deterred rather
than attracted

00:28:51.270 --> 00:28:53.400
by that sort of amplification.

00:28:53.400 --> 00:28:54.750
Men aren't frogs.

00:28:54.750 --> 00:28:58.740
They don't want women who are
10 times bigger than them.

00:28:58.740 --> 00:29:02.200
'Female artists are biologically
confused,' said Crake.

00:29:06.900 --> 00:29:11.250
'You must have
discovered that by now.'

00:29:11.250 --> 00:29:15.810
This was a snide dig at
Jimmy's current snarled romance

00:29:15.810 --> 00:29:20.010
with a brunette poet who
had renamed herself Morgana,

00:29:20.010 --> 00:29:23.760
and refused to tell him what
her given name had been,

00:29:23.760 --> 00:29:26.550
and who was currently
on a 28-day sex

00:29:26.550 --> 00:29:30.900
fast in honor of the
great moon goddess

00:29:30.900 --> 00:29:34.485
Eostre, patroness of
soy beans and bunnies.

00:29:37.260 --> 00:29:41.550
Martha Graham attracted
those kinds of girls.

00:29:41.550 --> 00:29:48.442
An error though to have
confided this affair to Crake."

00:29:48.442 --> 00:29:51.893
[APPLAUSE]

00:29:58.320 --> 00:30:00.570
That's from Crake's past.

00:30:00.570 --> 00:30:03.760
I'll read you a small
bit from his present,

00:30:03.760 --> 00:30:07.410
and then we can have a
question-and-answer session

00:30:07.410 --> 00:30:11.110
in which you can ask me
things you are dying to know,

00:30:11.110 --> 00:30:14.162
such as, how come
you're a blonde now?

00:30:14.162 --> 00:30:16.622
[LAUGHTER]

00:30:24.510 --> 00:30:27.570
So this is from--

00:30:27.570 --> 00:30:31.710
Jimmy is-- Jimmy Snowman
is wearing his bed sheet,

00:30:31.710 --> 00:30:34.620
and sitting on the
beach, and it's dusk.

00:30:34.620 --> 00:30:41.760
And he's remembering that little
poem he was taught as a child.

00:30:41.760 --> 00:30:45.560
"Snowman screws his eyes shut,
pushes his fists into them,

00:30:45.560 --> 00:30:48.020
clenches his entire face.

00:30:48.020 --> 00:30:50.390
There is the wishing
star, all right.

00:30:50.390 --> 00:30:52.130
It's blue.

00:30:52.130 --> 00:30:54.650
'I wish I may, I wish
I might," he says,

00:30:54.650 --> 00:30:57.740
'have the wish I wish tonight."

00:30:57.740 --> 00:31:00.660
Fat chance.

00:31:00.660 --> 00:31:03.395
"Oh, Snowman, why are you
talking to no one?' says

00:31:03.395 --> 00:31:04.390
a voice.

00:31:04.390 --> 00:31:06.260
Snowman opens his eyes.

00:31:06.260 --> 00:31:09.770
Three of the older children
are standing just out of reach,

00:31:09.770 --> 00:31:11.570
regarding him with interest.

00:31:11.570 --> 00:31:15.320
They must have crept
up on him in the dusk.

00:31:15.320 --> 00:31:18.050
'I'm talking to Crake,' he says.

00:31:18.050 --> 00:31:20.690
'But you talk to Crake
through your shiny thing.

00:31:20.690 --> 00:31:22.350
Is it broken?'

00:31:22.350 --> 00:31:26.060
Snowman lifts his left
arm, holds out his watch.

00:31:26.060 --> 00:31:28.580
'This is for listening to Crake.

00:31:28.580 --> 00:31:31.740
Talking to him is different.'

00:31:31.740 --> 00:31:34.370
'Why are you talking
to him about stars?

00:31:34.370 --> 00:31:38.390
What are you telling
to Crake, oh, Snowman?'

00:31:38.390 --> 00:31:40.160
'I was telling
him,' says Snowman,

00:31:40.160 --> 00:31:42.920
'that you ask too
many questions.'

00:31:42.920 --> 00:31:44.980
He holds his watch to his ear.

00:31:44.980 --> 00:31:48.770
'And he's telling me that if
you don't stop doing that,

00:31:48.770 --> 00:31:51.920
you'll be toast.'

00:31:51.920 --> 00:31:56.660
'Please, oh, Snowman,
what is toast?'

00:31:56.660 --> 00:31:58.640
Another error, Snowman thinks.

00:31:58.640 --> 00:32:01.790
He should avoid
arcane metaphors.

00:32:01.790 --> 00:32:07.420
'Toast,' he says, 'is
something very, very bad.

00:32:07.420 --> 00:32:11.360
It's so bad, I can't
even describe it.

00:32:11.360 --> 00:32:13.070
Now it's your bedtime.

00:32:13.070 --> 00:32:15.410
Go away.

00:32:15.410 --> 00:32:20.480
What is toast?' says Snowman to
himself once they've run off.

00:32:20.480 --> 00:32:23.370
Toast is when you
take a piece of bread.

00:32:23.370 --> 00:32:24.250
What is bread?

00:32:24.250 --> 00:32:26.000
Bread is when you
take some flour.

00:32:26.000 --> 00:32:26.720
What is flour?

00:32:26.720 --> 00:32:27.680
We'll skip that part.

00:32:27.680 --> 00:32:30.050
It's too complicated.

00:32:30.050 --> 00:32:33.530
Bread is something you can eat
made from a ground-up plant

00:32:33.530 --> 00:32:35.280
and shaped like a stone.

00:32:35.280 --> 00:32:36.180
You cook it.

00:32:36.180 --> 00:32:37.550
Please, why do you cook it?

00:32:37.550 --> 00:32:39.140
Why don't you just
eat the plant?

00:32:39.140 --> 00:32:40.190
Never mind that part.

00:32:40.190 --> 00:32:41.510
Pay attention.

00:32:41.510 --> 00:32:42.470
You cook it.

00:32:42.470 --> 00:32:46.820
And then you cut it into
slices, and you put a slice

00:32:46.820 --> 00:32:52.010
into a toaster, which
is a metal box that

00:32:52.010 --> 00:32:55.280
heats up with electricity.

00:32:55.280 --> 00:32:56.780
What is electricity?

00:32:56.780 --> 00:32:59.030
Don't worry about that.

00:32:59.030 --> 00:33:01.430
While the slice
is in the toaster,

00:33:01.430 --> 00:33:03.920
you get out the butter.

00:33:03.920 --> 00:33:08.525
Butter is a yellow grease
made from the mammary glands

00:33:08.525 --> 00:33:10.530
of-- skip the butter.

00:33:10.530 --> 00:33:16.220
So the toaster turns
the slice of bread

00:33:16.220 --> 00:33:21.170
black on both sides
with smoke coming out.

00:33:21.170 --> 00:33:24.830
And then this toaster shoots
the slice up into the air,

00:33:24.830 --> 00:33:27.640
and it falls onto the floor.

00:33:27.640 --> 00:33:29.720
'Oh, forget it,' says Snowman.

00:33:29.720 --> 00:33:31.850
'Let's try again.'

00:33:31.850 --> 00:33:38.330
Toast was a pointless
invention from the dark ages.

00:33:38.330 --> 00:33:41.640
Toast was an
implement of torture

00:33:41.640 --> 00:33:44.330
that caused all
those subjected to it

00:33:44.330 --> 00:33:47.840
to regurgitate in
verbal form the sins

00:33:47.840 --> 00:33:51.320
and crimes of their past lives.

00:33:51.320 --> 00:33:56.420
Toast was a ritual item devoured
by fetishists in the belief

00:33:56.420 --> 00:34:00.920
that it would enhance their
kinetic and sexual powers.

00:34:00.920 --> 00:34:07.160
Toast cannot be explained
by any rational means.

00:34:07.160 --> 00:34:10.340
Toast is me.

00:34:10.340 --> 00:34:14.548
I am toast."

00:34:14.548 --> 00:34:17.500
[APPLAUSE]

00:34:27.360 --> 00:34:30.750
And we can do questions by
you waving your arm around,

00:34:30.750 --> 00:34:32.500
and then I'll repeat
it into the mic

00:34:32.500 --> 00:34:34.800
so people will know
what you asked,

00:34:34.800 --> 00:34:37.560
which will also give me
a chance to reformulate

00:34:37.560 --> 00:34:41.350
the question too.

00:34:41.350 --> 00:34:42.797
Yes.

00:34:42.797 --> 00:34:45.130
AUDIENCE: I work at the
National Organization for Women.

00:34:45.130 --> 00:34:46.838
And I'm constantly
quoting The Handmaid's

00:34:46.838 --> 00:34:50.670
Tale right now,
mainly in reference

00:34:50.670 --> 00:34:53.733
to the series of
attacks on Roe v. Wade.

00:34:53.733 --> 00:34:55.769
But when I read
Oryx and Crake, it

00:34:55.769 --> 00:34:57.720
started haunting
me in another way.

00:34:57.720 --> 00:35:01.290
So I wanted to ask
you, between the two,

00:35:01.290 --> 00:35:04.570
which future do you think
is more likely right now,

00:35:04.570 --> 00:35:06.767
and which is more frightening?

00:35:06.767 --> 00:35:08.555
ATWOOD: [CHUCKLES]

00:35:10.350 --> 00:35:13.260
All right, the question was,
there is another novel called

00:35:13.260 --> 00:35:16.740
The Handmaid's Tale, which
was published in 1985

00:35:16.740 --> 00:35:20.700
and which projects a
future in which the United

00:35:20.700 --> 00:35:25.550
States of America has become
a totalitarian theocracy.

00:35:25.550 --> 00:35:27.881
And for that--

00:35:27.881 --> 00:35:29.805
[LAUGHTER]

00:35:29.805 --> 00:35:32.210
[APPLAUSE]

00:35:35.580 --> 00:35:37.710
And that book came
out of my interest

00:35:37.710 --> 00:35:46.110
in world history, American
history, clothing laws,

00:35:46.110 --> 00:35:52.620
and such things, and also my
irritation when people say,

00:35:52.620 --> 00:35:54.780
it can't happen here.

00:35:54.780 --> 00:35:56.980
You know, that always
annoys me very much

00:35:56.980 --> 00:35:59.220
because anything, given
the right circumstances,

00:35:59.220 --> 00:36:01.350
can happen anywhere.

00:36:01.350 --> 00:36:05.700
What are the circumstances under
which totalitarianisms usually

00:36:05.700 --> 00:36:07.800
come in--

00:36:07.800 --> 00:36:10.170
social disruption
often connected

00:36:10.170 --> 00:36:15.540
with economic
disruption and fear

00:36:15.540 --> 00:36:20.310
so that people are
frightened, and confused,

00:36:20.310 --> 00:36:22.710
and they are at a
point where they're

00:36:22.710 --> 00:36:26.340
willing to trade their freedoms
for something they think

00:36:26.340 --> 00:36:29.950
will be safety and security.

00:36:29.950 --> 00:36:35.460
So that is one possible
form of frightening future.

00:36:35.460 --> 00:36:38.730
And my rule for that book
was I put nothing into it

00:36:38.730 --> 00:36:41.830
that human beings hadn't already
done at some time or another.

00:36:41.830 --> 00:36:45.750
So we know that this is
behavior that we are capable of.

00:36:45.750 --> 00:36:49.230
And I also am one
of those people

00:36:49.230 --> 00:36:53.730
who believe that you should
listen to what people say

00:36:53.730 --> 00:36:57.510
they're going to do if they
get into power because they

00:36:57.510 --> 00:37:00.520
probably will do it.

00:37:00.520 --> 00:37:01.560
OK?

00:37:01.560 --> 00:37:03.470
And one of the
mistakes with Hitler

00:37:03.470 --> 00:37:06.290
was that people read Mein Kampf,
and they thought, oh, he's

00:37:06.290 --> 00:37:08.120
just fooling--

00:37:08.120 --> 00:37:10.640
wrong.

00:37:10.640 --> 00:37:14.870
So I was born in 1939, the year
Canada went into World War II,

00:37:14.870 --> 00:37:19.160
so I've always been interested
in those kinds of histories.

00:37:19.160 --> 00:37:22.790
That's one kind of a
frightening future.

00:37:22.790 --> 00:37:26.510
And I did set it in Cambridge,
Massachusetts, which

00:37:26.510 --> 00:37:28.250
annoyed Harvard at the time.

00:37:32.210 --> 00:37:34.160
But that would be
the right place

00:37:34.160 --> 00:37:37.430
to set such a book because
"it can't happen here"

00:37:37.430 --> 00:37:40.970
should be placed in
the most extreme here.

00:37:40.970 --> 00:37:42.770
They were, I think,
somewhat perturbed

00:37:42.770 --> 00:37:46.010
that the Widener Library became
the home of the Secret Service.

00:37:46.010 --> 00:37:50.300
But they didn't
think it was funny.

00:37:50.300 --> 00:37:50.910
Oh, well.

00:37:53.912 --> 00:37:55.370
One of my friends
wrote me a letter

00:37:55.370 --> 00:37:57.148
and said, hasn't
anybody figured out yet

00:37:57.148 --> 00:37:58.940
that this book is about
the Harvard English

00:37:58.940 --> 00:38:04.940
Department, which has
changed its ways since.

00:38:04.940 --> 00:38:08.180
But at that time, it
didn't hire women.

00:38:08.180 --> 00:38:09.480
I'm really old.

00:38:09.480 --> 00:38:12.770
So, OK, between
that and this book,

00:38:12.770 --> 00:38:14.363
well, what is more frightening?

00:38:14.363 --> 00:38:16.280
I would say that this
book is more frightening

00:38:16.280 --> 00:38:20.810
because when you're talking
about political systems,

00:38:20.810 --> 00:38:25.730
we know that they can
be overcome and changed.

00:38:25.730 --> 00:38:28.310
And that's how The
Handmaid's Tale ends.

00:38:28.310 --> 00:38:30.830
It ends with a section
that takes place

00:38:30.830 --> 00:38:35.510
after the time of the book
in which that system is over.

00:38:35.510 --> 00:38:37.730
And I am, therefore,
one of those people

00:38:37.730 --> 00:38:41.570
who have always thought
that George Orwell's 1984 is

00:38:41.570 --> 00:38:45.140
a more cheerful book than
some people have supposed.

00:38:45.140 --> 00:38:47.330
Because what you
remember is the end

00:38:47.330 --> 00:38:51.350
of it, and with Winston Smith in
a comatose state, and the boot

00:38:51.350 --> 00:38:53.870
grinding into the
human face forever.

00:38:53.870 --> 00:38:56.120
But how the book really
ends is with a note

00:38:56.120 --> 00:38:58.850
on Newspeak written
in standard English

00:38:58.850 --> 00:39:02.720
in the past tense, which
means that the world of 1984

00:39:02.720 --> 00:39:04.710
has been finished.

00:39:04.710 --> 00:39:08.705
OK, so that is a more
hopeful scenario.

00:39:08.705 --> 00:39:12.620
The one in this
book brings together

00:39:12.620 --> 00:39:16.020
environmental catastrophe,
which we're already--

00:39:16.020 --> 00:39:17.435
excuse me-- heading towards.

00:39:21.090 --> 00:39:23.000
Not senile yet.

00:39:23.000 --> 00:39:25.535
Can still open bottle of water--

00:39:25.535 --> 00:39:26.035
cheering.

00:39:30.820 --> 00:39:35.600
It brings together environmental
catastrophe on the one hand

00:39:35.600 --> 00:39:39.140
and our scrabbling
attempts to do something

00:39:39.140 --> 00:39:42.550
about the diminishing
food supply on the other.

00:39:42.550 --> 00:39:48.030
We are due to peak at 9
or 10 billion in 2050.

00:39:48.030 --> 00:39:52.340
We have run through the 90%
of the world's fish stocks

00:39:52.340 --> 00:39:55.670
in the past 50 years.

00:39:55.670 --> 00:39:57.590
That's why Jimmy
in the cafeteria

00:39:57.590 --> 00:40:00.800
has fish finger, 20% real fish.

00:40:00.800 --> 00:40:04.670
I'm now thinking
that 20% was high.

00:40:04.670 --> 00:40:06.770
Interesting question,
what is the other 80%?

00:40:09.410 --> 00:40:13.100
So those things are on the road.

00:40:13.100 --> 00:40:16.460
And I think we're past the
point where even governments

00:40:16.460 --> 00:40:22.400
can be global warming deniers
as they have managed to do

00:40:22.400 --> 00:40:25.470
so well for so many years.

00:40:25.470 --> 00:40:31.610
So I'd say this one is scarier
although it's not completely

00:40:31.610 --> 00:40:35.750
game over for the human
race, even in this book,

00:40:35.750 --> 00:40:41.330
as we discover with mixed
feelings towards the end.

00:40:46.480 --> 00:40:46.980
Yes.

00:40:46.980 --> 00:40:49.680
AUDIENCE: How do you see
the Crakers evolving?

00:40:49.680 --> 00:40:52.510
ATWOOD: How do I see
the Crakers evolving?

00:40:52.510 --> 00:40:54.600
Well, their creator
has tried very

00:40:54.600 --> 00:40:59.710
hard to get rid of a few
things in them without success.

00:40:59.710 --> 00:41:04.920
For instance, they still
are avid question-askers.

00:41:04.920 --> 00:41:09.540
Someone has defined human beings
as the animal that asks "why?"

00:41:09.540 --> 00:41:11.580
So they still do that.

00:41:11.580 --> 00:41:14.010
He's tried to get
rid of singing,

00:41:14.010 --> 00:41:16.440
but he couldn't do that either.

00:41:16.440 --> 00:41:20.688
Music, I think it seems
to be quite hardwired.

00:41:20.688 --> 00:41:22.230
And he's trying to
get rid of dreams,

00:41:22.230 --> 00:41:23.640
and he couldn't do that.

00:41:23.640 --> 00:41:28.650
So they still make music, have
dreams, and asked questions.

00:41:28.650 --> 00:41:30.150
And where will that lead?

00:41:34.210 --> 00:41:37.560
I certainly wouldn't want
to narrow the possibilities

00:41:37.560 --> 00:41:39.840
by giving my own opinion.

00:41:39.840 --> 00:41:45.330
Let us say that they do start
making something suspiciously--

00:41:45.330 --> 00:41:50.100
something suspicious
towards the end of the book.

00:41:50.100 --> 00:41:50.608
Yes.

00:41:50.608 --> 00:41:52.150
AUDIENCE: Can you
tell us a bit about

00:41:52.150 --> 00:41:55.430
was it was like for you as a
new writer writing a first novel

00:41:55.430 --> 00:41:56.578
and becoming published?

00:41:56.578 --> 00:41:58.620
ATWOOD: What it was like
for a new writer writing

00:41:58.620 --> 00:42:01.770
my first novel and
becoming published--

00:42:01.770 --> 00:42:04.770
I started writing when
I was 16 in high school

00:42:04.770 --> 00:42:07.560
because I knew no better.

00:42:07.560 --> 00:42:15.100
And I did not know actually
how hard it was going to be.

00:42:15.100 --> 00:42:18.820
I was quite sanguine
about the whole thing.

00:42:18.820 --> 00:42:20.770
I actually thought--
and this is true.

00:42:20.770 --> 00:42:24.670
I thought I could write true
romance stories in the day

00:42:24.670 --> 00:42:27.100
because you could make
money out of those,

00:42:27.100 --> 00:42:28.780
and I would sell those.

00:42:28.780 --> 00:42:31.570
And then in the evenings,
I would write my works

00:42:31.570 --> 00:42:35.050
of blazing artistic genius.

00:42:35.050 --> 00:42:39.370
And I did try a couple of
those true romance stories,

00:42:39.370 --> 00:42:41.500
but I could not do them--

00:42:41.500 --> 00:42:42.910
not because of the plots.

00:42:42.910 --> 00:42:44.410
I was good at the plots.

00:42:44.410 --> 00:42:46.770
I know what the basic plot is.

00:42:46.770 --> 00:42:49.420
It was, in those days,
girl meets two boys.

00:42:49.420 --> 00:42:50.860
One of them works
in a shoe store.

00:42:50.860 --> 00:42:52.714
The other has a motorcycle.

00:42:52.714 --> 00:42:54.610
[LAUGHTER]

00:42:56.980 --> 00:43:00.020
She's gets tangled up with
the one with the motorcycle,

00:43:00.020 --> 00:43:03.290
and they have an
episode on the sofa.

00:43:03.290 --> 00:43:08.400
And in those days, it had
to be phrased as such--

00:43:08.400 --> 00:43:12.470
and then they were one,
dot, dot, dot, dot.

00:43:12.470 --> 00:43:16.040
Well, that was the
part I couldn't do.

00:43:16.040 --> 00:43:18.830
I couldn't do the dots.

00:43:18.830 --> 00:43:20.810
After that, he rides
off on his motorcycle,

00:43:20.810 --> 00:43:22.670
and she has remorse.

00:43:22.670 --> 00:43:24.395
And if the story
has a happy ending,

00:43:24.395 --> 00:43:25.770
she marries the
guy from the shoe

00:43:25.770 --> 00:43:28.790
store, who forgives her
for everything or else just

00:43:28.790 --> 00:43:29.370
isn't told.

00:43:33.800 --> 00:43:36.605
So I could do those OK.

00:43:36.605 --> 00:43:37.980
So that wasn't
going to work out.

00:43:37.980 --> 00:43:40.880
So I was going to
be a journalist.

00:43:40.880 --> 00:43:43.930
My parents bit their tongues.

00:43:43.930 --> 00:43:46.610
They had seen a wonderful
career in botany

00:43:46.610 --> 00:43:49.290
for me because I was
quite good at it.

00:43:49.290 --> 00:43:51.110
And if I had gone
in that direction,

00:43:51.110 --> 00:43:55.610
I would be cloning your glow
in the dark potatoes right now.

00:44:00.110 --> 00:44:03.260
So they dredged up some
second cousin who told me,

00:44:03.260 --> 00:44:08.030
at that time, the '50s, if
you become a woman journalist

00:44:08.030 --> 00:44:11.420
and work for a newspaper, you
will only write two things,

00:44:11.420 --> 00:44:16.810
the wedding write-ups
and the obituaries--

00:44:16.810 --> 00:44:18.870
good training for
a novelist anyway.

00:44:18.870 --> 00:44:26.490
But so I went into
honors English instead.

00:44:26.490 --> 00:44:29.450
That way, I could
teach at a university.

00:44:29.450 --> 00:44:34.280
And then in the summers, I
could write my towering works

00:44:34.280 --> 00:44:37.860
of blazing artistic genius.

00:44:37.860 --> 00:44:40.940
So I did take two
years off in the middle

00:44:40.940 --> 00:44:44.300
because I was scared of
my Latin exam at Harvard.

00:44:44.300 --> 00:44:46.440
And I worked in a
market research company

00:44:46.440 --> 00:44:48.260
and lived in a closet.

00:44:48.260 --> 00:44:50.660
And it was in a rooming house.

00:44:50.660 --> 00:44:51.950
They had those then.

00:44:51.950 --> 00:44:56.890
And I wrote my first novel,
which was rejected by everyone.

00:44:56.890 --> 00:44:59.750
I also wrote a book of
poems, and this is worse.

00:44:59.750 --> 00:45:02.510
It was accepted.

00:45:02.510 --> 00:45:04.730
I told all my friends.

00:45:04.730 --> 00:45:08.210
And then by the third member
of the small literary press,

00:45:08.210 --> 00:45:09.740
it was rejected.

00:45:09.740 --> 00:45:11.840
How depressing is that?

00:45:11.840 --> 00:45:12.680
What did I do?

00:45:12.680 --> 00:45:13.940
I got engaged.

00:45:13.940 --> 00:45:21.080
And, well, who can blame me?

00:45:21.080 --> 00:45:23.750
After that, I was going
to run off to Europe,

00:45:23.750 --> 00:45:26.450
live in a garret, and
wear black clothing,

00:45:26.450 --> 00:45:28.880
drink absinthe,
smoke cigarettes,

00:45:28.880 --> 00:45:35.340
write towering works of blazing
artistic genius while working

00:45:35.340 --> 00:45:38.930
as a waitress in the day times.

00:45:38.930 --> 00:45:40.783
Well, that plan came to nothing.

00:45:40.783 --> 00:45:42.450
First of all, I
couldn't do the smoking.

00:45:42.450 --> 00:45:44.520
It made me cough.

00:45:44.520 --> 00:45:46.140
I couldn't do the drinking.

00:45:46.140 --> 00:45:47.940
It made me throw up.

00:45:47.940 --> 00:45:51.150
I have New England Puritan
ancestors, so it's genetic.

00:45:54.360 --> 00:45:56.600
So I went to graduate school.

00:45:56.600 --> 00:45:58.950
[LANGUAGE]

00:45:58.950 --> 00:46:00.830
[APPLAUSE]

00:46:02.720 --> 00:46:05.850
That way, I could--
well, you know the story.

00:46:05.850 --> 00:46:10.290
So then I got a teaching job at
University of British Columbia.

00:46:10.290 --> 00:46:11.280
And what did I teach?

00:46:11.280 --> 00:46:13.130
I was at the lowest rung.

00:46:13.130 --> 00:46:17.376
I taught grammar to
engineering students--

00:46:17.376 --> 00:46:19.240
[LAUGHTER]

00:46:22.040 --> 00:46:27.720
--at 8:30 in the morning
in a Quonset hut.

00:46:27.720 --> 00:46:31.140
And they had Quonset huts
left over from World War II.

00:46:31.140 --> 00:46:33.380
And we were all
asleep, so it was OK.

00:46:33.380 --> 00:46:36.240
But I did quite a
good thing for them.

00:46:36.240 --> 00:46:39.620
I made them read the
Parables of Kafka,

00:46:39.620 --> 00:46:42.550
useful to them in
their future work,

00:46:42.550 --> 00:46:47.600
and write short
prose pieces based

00:46:47.600 --> 00:46:50.690
on those, which they quite liked
doing because they had a puzzle

00:46:50.690 --> 00:46:51.560
element.

00:46:51.560 --> 00:46:55.010
And then we could work
on the grammar that way.

00:46:55.010 --> 00:46:57.450
And I also taught the
"Whistle Stop" chorus,

00:46:57.450 --> 00:46:59.353
Chaucer to TS Eliot--

00:46:59.353 --> 00:47:01.800
whew, like that.

00:47:01.800 --> 00:47:07.520
And while I was there, I wrote
my first published novel--

00:47:07.520 --> 00:47:09.060
not the one that was rejected.

00:47:09.060 --> 00:47:10.320
It was good it was rejected.

00:47:10.320 --> 00:47:12.410
It wasn't a very good novel--

00:47:12.410 --> 00:47:13.460
The Edible Woman.

00:47:13.460 --> 00:47:19.700
And I wrote it on
UBC exam booklets,

00:47:19.700 --> 00:47:23.370
which have a handy lined page
down the right hand and then

00:47:23.370 --> 00:47:27.300
a place where you can do
the doodling on the left.

00:47:27.300 --> 00:47:30.310
So those were the
days before computers.

00:47:30.310 --> 00:47:35.200
I then typed all of this onto my
typewriter-- with my typewriter

00:47:35.200 --> 00:47:37.790
onto pieces of paper.

00:47:37.790 --> 00:47:40.400
I've never been able to type.

00:47:40.400 --> 00:47:43.430
I'm so glad they
invented computers

00:47:43.430 --> 00:47:46.910
because I no longer have to
use the little white bottle

00:47:46.910 --> 00:47:49.970
with the brush and
the little white lines

00:47:49.970 --> 00:47:52.430
that you can type and stick on.

00:47:52.430 --> 00:47:53.720
All of that is gone.

00:47:53.720 --> 00:48:01.130
Now I have the cheerful man
in the box who waves at me.

00:48:01.130 --> 00:48:03.830
All I need, if I'm feeling
lonely, all I need to do

00:48:03.830 --> 00:48:07.910
is pretend I'm writing a letter.

00:48:07.910 --> 00:48:09.830
[LAUGHTER]

00:48:11.750 --> 00:48:13.290
Would you like some help?

00:48:17.560 --> 00:48:20.960
I can't get Bob the paper
clip on my computer.

00:48:20.960 --> 00:48:22.950
But I've been told about him.

00:48:22.950 --> 00:48:24.700
Anyway, so that's what I did.

00:48:24.700 --> 00:48:25.870
I typed it all up.

00:48:25.870 --> 00:48:31.390
And then I sent it into
the publisher, who lost it.

00:48:31.390 --> 00:48:32.695
This is a Canadian story.

00:48:35.220 --> 00:48:36.918
I actually got an
acceptance letter,

00:48:36.918 --> 00:48:37.960
and then I heard nothing.

00:48:37.960 --> 00:48:40.450
But I was studying for my
orals, and I knew nothing

00:48:40.450 --> 00:48:42.670
about publishing
anyway, so I didn't

00:48:42.670 --> 00:48:45.760
know why it was taking so long.

00:48:45.760 --> 00:48:51.460
And by the time I came out of
my trance, my orals trance,

00:48:51.460 --> 00:48:52.420
I wrote them a letter.

00:48:52.420 --> 00:48:54.670
By that time, I had
published a book of poetry

00:48:54.670 --> 00:48:57.430
and won a major literary award.

00:48:57.430 --> 00:49:00.280
And I said, where is my novel?

00:49:03.220 --> 00:49:05.490
Then I got a letter
from them saying,

00:49:05.490 --> 00:49:07.810
we read something
in the paper about

00:49:07.810 --> 00:49:09.250
your major literary prize.

00:49:09.250 --> 00:49:10.780
And it says you've
written a novel.

00:49:13.360 --> 00:49:14.340
Could we see it?

00:49:19.210 --> 00:49:21.430
Then the publisher took
me out and had a drink.

00:49:21.430 --> 00:49:23.410
He had actually five drinks.

00:49:23.410 --> 00:49:26.608
And he told me the
most outrageous lie.

00:49:26.608 --> 00:49:28.650
He would never be able to
get away with this now.

00:49:28.650 --> 00:49:31.300
But what he told me was
that the manuscript had

00:49:31.300 --> 00:49:35.920
been in the hands of a woman
editor who had gotten pregnant,

00:49:35.920 --> 00:49:40.080
and you know how that makes
them go funny in the head.

00:49:40.080 --> 00:49:43.900
And she had left it in a
drawer without telling anyone,

00:49:43.900 --> 00:49:46.960
and that's why they hadn't
been answering my letters.

00:49:46.960 --> 00:49:48.490
What a whopper.

00:49:48.490 --> 00:49:52.060
It was all the time on his
very own floor covered up

00:49:52.060 --> 00:49:53.870
with other manuscripts.

00:49:53.870 --> 00:49:57.100
So that's my publishing
story about my first novel,

00:49:57.100 --> 00:50:02.710
which came out in 1969 although
I'd written it in '64 or '65.

00:50:02.710 --> 00:50:05.530
And I did my very,
very first book signing

00:50:05.530 --> 00:50:11.080
in the men's sock and underwear
department of the Hudson's Bay

00:50:11.080 --> 00:50:13.500
Company in Edmonton, Alberta.

00:50:17.240 --> 00:50:18.050
So there I was.

00:50:18.050 --> 00:50:18.550
It was--

00:50:18.550 --> 00:50:19.000
AUDIENCE: Really?

00:50:19.000 --> 00:50:19.690
ATWOOD: Yes.

00:50:19.690 --> 00:50:21.260
I don't know why that happened.

00:50:21.260 --> 00:50:23.870
It was the publicist's
first week on the job.

00:50:23.870 --> 00:50:24.875
Do we--

00:50:24.875 --> 00:50:25.750
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:50:25.750 --> 00:50:27.970
ATWOOD: Do we know how
long she lasted after that?

00:50:27.970 --> 00:50:29.170
Actually, no.

00:50:29.170 --> 00:50:32.560
But there I was with my little
pile of books saying The Edible

00:50:32.560 --> 00:50:35.024
Woman.

00:50:35.024 --> 00:50:38.200
And here are these guys
coming in in their galoshes

00:50:38.200 --> 00:50:42.730
because it was winter, looking
to snaffle a pair of jockey

00:50:42.730 --> 00:50:45.070
shorts during their lunch hour.

00:50:45.070 --> 00:50:50.650
And they took one look at
me and ran the other way.

00:50:50.650 --> 00:50:51.970
I think I sold two copies.

00:50:51.970 --> 00:50:56.320
And I thought, is this the
glamorous literary life?

00:50:56.320 --> 00:50:58.810
Well, it did get more
glamorous after that.

00:50:58.810 --> 00:51:02.620
I can't remember when that was.

00:51:02.620 --> 00:51:06.032
Anything else
you'd like to know?

00:51:06.032 --> 00:51:08.500
AUDIENCE: Do you have any
words of encouragement?

00:51:08.500 --> 00:51:12.430
ATWOOD: Words of encouragement--
don't throw anything out.

00:51:12.430 --> 00:51:16.540
You never know when it
might come in handy.

00:51:16.540 --> 00:51:19.570
How many manuscripts did
I submit before I finally

00:51:19.570 --> 00:51:20.530
got one published?

00:51:20.530 --> 00:51:22.810
I used to submit a
book of poems a year,

00:51:22.810 --> 00:51:26.090
and that went on for some time.

00:51:26.090 --> 00:51:31.390
So I think probably "keep at
it" is the word of encouragement

00:51:31.390 --> 00:51:34.180
you're looking for.

00:51:34.180 --> 00:51:36.700
As for the writing
part, don't look down.

00:51:39.250 --> 00:51:42.250
Just pretend you're crossing
Niagara on a tightrope.

00:51:42.250 --> 00:51:43.570
Don't look down.

00:51:43.570 --> 00:51:47.620
It's one step ahead, one
foot in front of the other.

00:51:47.620 --> 00:51:49.990
Don't worry about large issues.

00:51:49.990 --> 00:51:53.340
Worry about the page.

00:51:53.340 --> 00:51:56.050
The page is all you've got.

00:51:56.050 --> 00:51:56.800
Yes.

00:51:56.800 --> 00:51:58.950
AUDIENCE: Could you talk
about lack of closure

00:51:58.950 --> 00:52:01.685
in some of your
works, for example,

00:52:01.685 --> 00:52:05.420
in Death by Landscape
with Lois and Lucy

00:52:05.420 --> 00:52:07.090
and not finding closure--

00:52:07.090 --> 00:52:08.405
ATWOOD: Lack of closure--

00:52:08.405 --> 00:52:09.280
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:52:09.280 --> 00:52:12.100
ATWOOD: You mean not telling
people who had done it.

00:52:12.100 --> 00:52:12.965
AUDIENCE: Right.

00:52:12.965 --> 00:52:13.465
ATWOOD: Yes.

00:52:18.020 --> 00:52:21.980
Closure is very useful in
some kinds of writing--

00:52:21.980 --> 00:52:24.110
detective stories.

00:52:24.110 --> 00:52:28.310
You would feel very
ticked off if you

00:52:28.310 --> 00:52:32.070
read through the whole thing,
and there was no answer.

00:52:32.070 --> 00:52:34.160
You know, you'd
really feel annoyed

00:52:34.160 --> 00:52:36.680
because that's the kind
of book you want to read.

00:52:36.680 --> 00:52:39.530
You want to read one with
closure, which things

00:52:39.530 --> 00:52:43.320
get wrapped up at the end.

00:52:43.320 --> 00:52:48.240
I would like to write that
kind of book, but I don't.

00:52:48.240 --> 00:52:51.350
And I think I'd like to
leave a space at the end

00:52:51.350 --> 00:52:53.390
so that the reader can.

00:52:53.390 --> 00:52:58.190
Because I think that reading
is a very participatory kind

00:52:58.190 --> 00:52:59.580
of activity.

00:52:59.580 --> 00:53:01.550
In fact, when you
wire people up when

00:53:01.550 --> 00:53:03.530
they're reading, more
of their brain lights

00:53:03.530 --> 00:53:07.850
up a lot more than when
they're, guess what, watching TV

00:53:07.850 --> 00:53:13.340
because you have to participate
in the act of creating

00:53:13.340 --> 00:53:13.940
the story.

00:53:13.940 --> 00:53:21.950
It's, I would say, reading
is to the spoken word

00:53:21.950 --> 00:53:25.190
as a musical score
with somebody playing

00:53:25.190 --> 00:53:29.810
it is to the composition
that you hear.

00:53:29.810 --> 00:53:32.730
So the page is really
just a score for voice.

00:53:32.730 --> 00:53:36.230
And when you're reading
that page, you, the reader,

00:53:36.230 --> 00:53:38.000
are the musician.

00:53:38.000 --> 00:53:40.100
You're doing the interpretation.

00:53:40.100 --> 00:53:44.390
You're also doing the
costume design, the acting.

00:53:44.390 --> 00:53:46.590
You're doing all
of those things.

00:53:46.590 --> 00:53:49.140
So I like to leave
room for that.

00:53:49.140 --> 00:53:51.380
And I like to leave
room for the reader

00:53:51.380 --> 00:53:55.570
to join in the invention.

00:53:55.570 --> 00:53:59.376
And sometimes, I just can't
think of how it would end.

00:53:59.376 --> 00:54:00.360
I don't know.

00:54:04.790 --> 00:54:06.532
Yes.

00:54:06.532 --> 00:54:07.990
AUDIENCE: How might
do you consider

00:54:07.990 --> 00:54:11.610
your stories' engagement
with the culture of power

00:54:11.610 --> 00:54:14.860
or, let's say, in a culture of
American power given that you

00:54:14.860 --> 00:54:16.680
came out of a
Canadian background?

00:54:16.680 --> 00:54:19.390
And in that regard,
which of your stories

00:54:19.390 --> 00:54:21.805
do you think feels the
most Canadian to you?

00:54:21.805 --> 00:54:24.640
ATWOOD: OK, that's
a double question.

00:54:24.640 --> 00:54:28.270
How does my-- how do I
think my writing engages

00:54:28.270 --> 00:54:31.540
with the issue of
power, specifically

00:54:31.540 --> 00:54:33.610
American power,
by which I take it

00:54:33.610 --> 00:54:37.150
you mean American power
in the world or possibly

00:54:37.150 --> 00:54:42.160
American power within
America, number one.

00:54:42.160 --> 00:54:44.080
And number two,
which of my stories

00:54:44.080 --> 00:54:46.600
appeals most to Canadians?

00:54:46.600 --> 00:54:50.720
Well, there is a shocking
piece of news for you.

00:54:50.720 --> 00:54:55.150
First of all, not all
Americans are alike.

00:54:55.150 --> 00:54:56.800
And the other thing
that goes along

00:54:56.800 --> 00:54:58.940
with that is not all
Canadians are alike.

00:54:58.940 --> 00:55:00.700
So some of them like one thing.

00:55:00.700 --> 00:55:02.140
Some like another.

00:55:02.140 --> 00:55:05.770
They tended to be quite
keen on The Blind Assassin

00:55:05.770 --> 00:55:09.970
because it had a lot of history
in it that they recognized.

00:55:09.970 --> 00:55:13.920
But similarly, they
liked Cat's Eye,

00:55:13.920 --> 00:55:15.910
but so did a lot of people.

00:55:15.910 --> 00:55:18.370
And it was a generational thing.

00:55:18.370 --> 00:55:22.360
The power question,
to get back to that,

00:55:22.360 --> 00:55:26.650
I think all narratives
include an element that

00:55:26.650 --> 00:55:29.590
has to do with
power, that is, who's

00:55:29.590 --> 00:55:31.120
got the power in the story?

00:55:31.120 --> 00:55:32.630
Who's got less power?

00:55:32.630 --> 00:55:34.870
How does the crafty
person with less power

00:55:34.870 --> 00:55:40.660
go about fooling the
person with more power,

00:55:40.660 --> 00:55:44.240
and so on, and so forth.

00:55:44.240 --> 00:55:47.080
I don't know whether
there's a direct answer

00:55:47.080 --> 00:55:52.390
to your first question because
I'm not writing a directly,

00:55:52.390 --> 00:55:56.470
small p, political
kind of book, that

00:55:56.470 --> 00:55:58.600
is, I don't write about
the Republican Party,

00:55:58.600 --> 00:56:01.940
and the Democratic Party,
and those kinds of things.

00:56:01.940 --> 00:56:08.260
I think I write about attitudes,
and what those attitudes do

00:56:08.260 --> 00:56:10.540
to people in
relationships, and when

00:56:10.540 --> 00:56:13.920
they're transferred into big--

00:56:13.920 --> 00:56:17.230
when they become large
scale, what kinds of effects

00:56:17.230 --> 00:56:19.210
they can have on people.

00:56:19.210 --> 00:56:21.140
In The Handmaid's
Tale, for instance,

00:56:21.140 --> 00:56:24.370
there's a powerful
totalitarian government.

00:56:24.370 --> 00:56:27.970
In Oryx and Crake,
government has, more or less,

00:56:27.970 --> 00:56:31.630
as we know it today,
more or less vanished.

00:56:31.630 --> 00:56:36.520
And security issues are in
the hands of mercenary armies.

00:56:36.520 --> 00:56:40.190
Read any news lately?

00:56:40.190 --> 00:56:45.230
That's who's making a lot of
money in Iraq at the moment.

00:56:45.230 --> 00:56:49.150
They're English
mercenary armies.

00:56:49.150 --> 00:56:51.850
So that's as good an
answer as I can do.

00:56:51.850 --> 00:56:56.520
And I'll do one more, and
then we'll wind it up.

00:56:56.520 --> 00:56:59.430
AUDIENCE: I just want to ask,
what science fiction or what

00:56:59.430 --> 00:57:01.810
science fiction writers do
you think influenced you?

00:57:01.810 --> 00:57:04.320
ATWOOD: What science
fictions-- fiction writers

00:57:04.320 --> 00:57:06.420
do I think have influenced me?

00:57:06.420 --> 00:57:09.600
Funny you should ask.

00:57:09.600 --> 00:57:11.820
The list is long
because this was once

00:57:11.820 --> 00:57:16.150
part of what was going
to be my thesis topic.

00:57:16.150 --> 00:57:19.590
In fact, I got almost
through it before I

00:57:19.590 --> 00:57:23.100
realized that I didn't have
to teach in a university

00:57:23.100 --> 00:57:23.790
after all.

00:57:27.600 --> 00:57:32.220
I can write film scripts
that never got made--

00:57:32.220 --> 00:57:37.620
so, yes, the 19th-century
ones in particular.

00:57:37.620 --> 00:57:40.020
And watch for it
coming out soon,

00:57:40.020 --> 00:57:43.830
my introduction to The
Island of Dr. Moreau.

00:57:43.830 --> 00:57:46.410
And on the stands,
even as we speak,

00:57:46.410 --> 00:57:51.540
is my introduction to
Rider Haggard's She.

00:57:51.540 --> 00:57:54.870
So that will tell you
something about that.

00:57:54.870 --> 00:57:57.210
I'm not very
current, that is, I'm

00:57:57.210 --> 00:58:00.730
not up on absolutely everything
that's being done today.

00:58:00.730 --> 00:58:04.080
But if you want to read my
review of Ursula Le Guin,

00:58:04.080 --> 00:58:08.740
it was in The New York
Review of Books last year.

00:58:08.740 --> 00:58:11.310
And that has somewhat
of an overview,

00:58:11.310 --> 00:58:16.170
you know, where did science
fiction come from, et cetera--

00:58:16.170 --> 00:58:18.360
so all of the classics
including some

00:58:18.360 --> 00:58:19.980
of the ones you
have never heard of,

00:58:19.980 --> 00:58:25.320
such as WH Hudson and his
strange book A Crystal Age,

00:58:25.320 --> 00:58:31.170
and The Purple Cloud,
those kinds of books.

00:58:31.170 --> 00:58:35.280
I was very keen as a child on
The Lost World, Arthur Conan

00:58:35.280 --> 00:58:39.360
Doyle's book about discovering
a plateau full of dinosaurs.

00:58:39.360 --> 00:58:40.950
I really loved that.

00:58:40.950 --> 00:58:42.870
It's been made into
some really bad movies,

00:58:42.870 --> 00:58:46.650
but I liked it as a book.

00:58:46.650 --> 00:58:48.930
So if you want the full
reading list, we can talk.

00:58:48.930 --> 00:58:50.860
But it's long.

00:58:50.860 --> 00:58:55.110
And one of the things I probably
got from doing all of that

00:58:55.110 --> 00:58:59.960
is some ideas on what I
myself would like to avoid.

00:58:59.960 --> 00:59:01.816
You know?

00:59:01.816 --> 00:59:05.070
Because there are sometimes
parts in them that you think,

00:59:05.070 --> 00:59:10.710
we might have been
better off without.

00:59:10.710 --> 00:59:13.950
